n each deviation from the mean, squaring it, and preparing to divide by the sample size. This will help me confirm the accuracy of my earlier computation and ensure I'm using the correct statistical approach for this dataset.\n\n. 64 + 3737713702.24 + 263,157,600,132.84) / 5\n- = 11,893,814,939,200 / 5\n- = 2,378,762,987,840\n\nThe variance is extremely large, indicating significant spread in the data points. I'll continue tracking the standard deviation calculation to understand the dispersion more precisely.\n\nThe coefficient of variation comes out to 11.60%, suggesting moderate variability relative to the mean. This percentage represents the ratio of the standard deviation to the mean, providing a standardized measure of dispersion.\n\nI want to verify if the data source from the Treasury Bulletins matches my current calculations, as there might be nuances in how the fiscal year data is interpreted.\n","signature":""},{"type":"toolRequest","id":"call_41542a2343ff47a7945869ad","toolCall":{"status":"success","value":{"name":"todo__todo_write","arguments":{"content":"- [x] Find FFO-4 tables with Social Security Administration nominal budget outlay data\n- [x] Extract data from odd years (2011, 2013, 2015, 2017, 2019) December Treasury Bulletins\n- [x] Calculate mean and population standard deviation\n- [x] Compute coefficient of variation (CV = σ/μ × 100)\n- [x] Round to nearest hundredths place\n- [ ] Write answer to /app/answer.txt"}}},"_meta":{"goose_extension":"todo"}}],"metadata":{"userVisible":true,"agentVisible":true}}}
